---
import BaseLayout from '../layouts/BaseLayout.astro';
const faqSchema = {
 "@context": "https://schema.org",
 "@type": "FAQPage",
 "mainEntity": [
    {'@type': 'Question', 'name': "Why is port 25 blocked on my network?", "acceptedAnswer": { '@type': 'Answer', "text": "ISPs and cloud providers block it to prevent spam botnets from sending directly. Use 587/465 through your mail provider instead." } },
    {'@type': 'Question', 'name': "Is 465 more secure than 587?", "acceptedAnswer": { '@type': 'Answer', "text": "465 uses implicit TLS (encrypted immediately); 587 starts plaintext and upgrades. Both are secure when configured correctly; 465 avoids the downgrade window." } },
    {'@type': 'Question', 'name': "What port does Gmail use?", "acceptedAnswer": { '@type': 'Answer', "text": "587 for SMTP submission (smtp.gmail.com) with STARTTLS, or 465 with SSL. Both require authentication." } },
    {'@type': 'Question', 'name': "Can I run my own mail server on port 25?", "acceptedAnswer": { '@type': 'Answer', "text": "Yes, but your ISP must unblock it (business lines usually allow it; consumer lines don\u2019t). You also need correct PTR, SPF, and DKIM." } },
    {'@type': 'Question', 'name': "What happens if I use the wrong port?", "acceptedAnswer": { '@type': 'Answer', "text": "Connection refused or timeout: the server isn\u2019t listening on that port. The error message tells you which port to use." } },
    {'@type': 'Question', 'name': "Does the port affect deliverability?", "acceptedAnswer": { '@type': 'Answer', "text": "No: the port is just transport. Deliverability depends on authentication, reputation, and content." } }
  ]
}
---
<BaseLayout
  title="Email Ports Explained: 25 vs 465 vs 587"
  description="email ports 25 465 587"
  schema={[faqSchema]}
 breadcrumb={[["Home", "/"], ["Email Authentication", "/email-authentication/"], ["Email Ports Explained: 25 vs 465 vs 587"]]}>
  <div class="mx-auto max-w-3xl px-4 py-12">
    <nav class="mb-4 text-sm text-ink-faint" aria-label="Breadcrumb">
      <a href="/" class="hover:text-accent">Home</a> <span aria-hidden="true">/</span>
      <a href="/email-authentication/" class="hover:text-accent">Email Authentication</a> <span aria-hidden="true">/</span>
      <span class="text-ink-dim">Email Ports Explained: 25 vs 465 vs 587</span>
    </nav>
    <h1 class="font-display text-3xl font-bold tracking-tight text-ink sm:text-4xl">Email Ports Explained: 25 vs 465 vs 587</h1>
    <div class="pd-answer" data-vs="answer">
      <p class="text-lg leading-relaxed text-ink-dim">The three email ports serve different purposes: 25 for server-to-server relay, 587 for user submission with STARTTLS, and 465 for implicit TLS. Using the wrong one causes connection failures.</p>
    </div>
<nav class="pd-toc" aria-label="On this page">
      <p class="font-mono text-[10px] uppercase tracking-[0.18em] text-accent">On this page</p>
      <ul>
        <li><a href="#the-reference">The reference</a></li>
        <li><a href="#which-should-you-use">Which should you use?</a></li>
        <li><a href="#frequently-asked-questions">Frequently asked questions</a></li>
        <li><a href="#related">Related</a></li>
      </ul>
    </nav>
    <div class="rich-text mt-8">
      <h2 class="font-display mt-10 text-xl font-semibold text-ink" id="the-reference">The reference</h2>
      <div class="overflow-x-auto rounded-xl border border-line bg-panel/60"><table class="pdns-table"><tbody><tr><td class="text-sm text-ink-dim">25</td><td class="text-sm text-ink-dim">Server-to-server relay (MX delivery)</td><td class="text-sm text-ink-dim">Should NOT be used for user submission</td></tr><tr><td class="text-sm text-ink-dim">465</td><td class="text-sm text-ink-dim">Implicit TLS submission (user to server)</td><td class="text-sm text-ink-dim">Encrypted from the first byte; recommended</td></tr><tr><td class="text-sm text-ink-dim">587</td><td class="text-sm text-ink-dim">STARTTLS submission (user to server)</td><td class="text-sm text-ink-dim">Starts plaintext, upgrades to TLS; most common</td></tr></tbody></table></div>
      <h2 class="font-display mt-10 text-xl font-semibold text-ink" id="which-should-you-use">Which should you use?</h2>
      <ul class="list-disc space-y-1.5 pl-5"><li><strong class="text-ink">Configuring your email client:</strong> 587 or 465, with authentication (username + password or app password). Both are secure.</li><li><strong class="text-ink">Server-to-server delivery:</strong> port 25, handled automatically by mail servers using MX records.</li><li><strong class="text-ink">Never expose port 25 to user devices:</strong> it’s for infrastructure, and most ISPs block it on consumer lines.</li></ul>
      <h2 class="font-display mt-10 text-xl font-semibold text-ink" id="frequently-asked-questions">Frequently asked questions</h2>
      <div class="mt-3 grid gap-3"><details class="group rounded-xl border border-line bg-panel/60 px-5 py-4 open:border-accent/40"><summary class="flex cursor-pointer list-none items-center justify-between gap-4 font-semibold text-ink [&::-webkit-details-marker]:hidden">Why is port 25 blocked on my network?<span class="text-accent transition-transform duration-200 group-open:rotate-45">+</span></summary><p class="mt-3 text-sm leading-relaxed text-ink-dim">ISPs and cloud providers block it to prevent spam botnets from sending directly. Use 587/465 through your mail provider instead.</p></details><details class="group rounded-xl border border-line bg-panel/60 px-5 py-4 open:border-accent/40"><summary class="flex cursor-pointer list-none items-center justify-between gap-4 font-semibold text-ink [&::-webkit-details-marker]:hidden">Is 465 more secure than 587?<span class="text-accent transition-transform duration-200 group-open:rotate-45">+</span></summary><p class="mt-3 text-sm leading-relaxed text-ink-dim">465 uses implicit TLS (encrypted immediately); 587 starts plaintext and upgrades. Both are secure when configured correctly; 465 avoids the downgrade window.</p></details><details class="group rounded-xl border border-line bg-panel/60 px-5 py-4 open:border-accent/40"><summary class="flex cursor-pointer list-none items-center justify-between gap-4 font-semibold text-ink [&::-webkit-details-marker]:hidden">What port does Gmail use?<span class="text-accent transition-transform duration-200 group-open:rotate-45">+</span></summary><p class="mt-3 text-sm leading-relaxed text-ink-dim">587 for SMTP submission (smtp.gmail.com) with STARTTLS, or 465 with SSL. Both require authentication.</p></details><details class="group rounded-xl border border-line bg-panel/60 px-5 py-4 open:border-accent/40"><summary class="flex cursor-pointer list-none items-center justify-between gap-4 font-semibold text-ink [&::-webkit-details-marker]:hidden">Can I run my own mail server on port 25?<span class="text-accent transition-transform duration-200 group-open:rotate-45">+</span></summary><p class="mt-3 text-sm leading-relaxed text-ink-dim">Yes, but your ISP must unblock it (business lines usually allow it; consumer lines don’t). You also need correct PTR, SPF, and DKIM.</p></details><details class="group rounded-xl border border-line bg-panel/60 px-5 py-4 open:border-accent/40"><summary class="flex cursor-pointer list-none items-center justify-between gap-4 font-semibold text-ink [&::-webkit-details-marker]:hidden">What happens if I use the wrong port?<span class="text-accent transition-transform duration-200 group-open:rotate-45">+</span></summary><p class="mt-3 text-sm leading-relaxed text-ink-dim">Connection refused or timeout: the server isn’t listening on that port. The error message tells you which port to use.</p></details><details class="group rounded-xl border border-line bg-panel/60 px-5 py-4 open:border-accent/40"><summary class="flex cursor-pointer list-none items-center justify-between gap-4 font-semibold text-ink [&::-webkit-details-marker]:hidden">Does the port affect deliverability?<span class="text-accent transition-transform duration-200 group-open:rotate-45">+</span></summary><p class="mt-3 text-sm leading-relaxed text-ink-dim">No: the port is just transport. Deliverability depends on authentication, reputation, and content.</p></details></div>
      <h2 class="font-display mt-10 text-xl font-semibold text-ink" id="related">Related</h2>
      <ul class="mt-3 grid gap-2"><li><a href="/smtp-tester/" class="text-accent hover:text-accent-soft">SMTP preflight →</a></li><li><a href="/imap-vs-smtp/" class="text-accent hover:text-accent-soft">IMAP vs SMTP →</a></li><li><a href="/mx-record/" class="text-accent hover:text-accent-soft">MX records →</a></li></ul>
    </div>
  </div>
</BaseLayout>
